# VIGIL SaaS settle-on-completion drain — config for vigil-settle-drain.service   (MONEY path)
# Copy to ~/.config/vigil/settle-drain.env and fill in.
# =====================================================================
# IMPORTANT — paths in THIS file must be ABSOLUTE. systemd does NOT expand %h (or any %-specifier) inside an
# EnvironmentFile value; a "%h/..." here reaches the drain as the LITERAL string. For this MONEY drain the CLI
# refuses a relative / %-bearing --spool and a %-bearing seam base (the drain exits non-zero → a FAILED alarm)
# rather than silently settling NOTHING — a literal-%h base-dir would make the lease READ find nothing, so every
# completion becomes a no-op that is still archived unsettled (a completed run never charged / a cancel never
# refunded, permanently). The unit ships working %h-expanded DEFAULTS for the spool, seam base and scope (in
# vigil-settle-drain.service's Environment= directives, where %h DOES expand), so the only thing you must set
# below is the delegation.

# REQUIRED: the owner-signed OFFENSE_GOVERNANCE delegation JSON, scoped to the cust_ engagement slug this drain
# settles. ABSOLUTE path (replace /home/USER with this unit's home). The drain is fail-closed without it.
VIGIL_SETTLE_DELEGATION=/home/USER/.config/vigil/offense-governance-delegation.json

# OPTIONAL OVERRIDES (each defaults from the .service; override only with an ABSOLUTE path):
#   VIGIL_SETTLE_SPOOL     the settle spool whose incoming/ is drained (default %h/.sigil/spool/settle); the
#                          offense worker writes signed completion signals here.
#   VIGIL_BASE_DIR         the ONE credit-lease seam base — the published lease is READ and the settlement/
#                          tombstone WRITTEN here (default %h/.sigil/credit-lease). It MUST be the SAME value the
#                          cockpit (sigil-cockpit.service), the offense backup and the offense side use, or every
#                          settle is a no-op (`vigil doctor` refuses when they disagree). If outside ~/.sigil, add
#                          it to the unit's ReadWritePaths. (The former VIGIL_SETTLE_BASE_DIR is retired: if it
#                          is still set here and names a different directory, the drain REFUSES to run.)
#   VIGIL_SETTLE_SCOPE     the cust_ slug this drain is confined to; must match the delegation scope ("*" only if
#                          the delegation itself is wildcard-scoped — a mismatch fails LOUD at startup).
# VIGIL_SETTLE_SPOOL=/home/USER/.sigil/spool/settle
# VIGIL_BASE_DIR=/home/USER/.sigil/credit-lease
# VIGIL_SETTLE_SCOPE=*

# NOTE: the dead-man ENABLE flag does NOT belong here. VIGIL_SETTLE_DRAIN_ENABLED is read by the vigil-alerts
# MONITOR process, not by this drain — set it in the monitor's env (~/.config/vigil/alerts.env), never in this
# file (where nothing reads it). It is only an EARLY-ARM: once the drain has fired once, its heartbeat arms the
# dead-man automatically. TEARDOWN: to turn the drain OFF, disable the timer, remove the flag from alerts.env,
# AND remove its heartbeat ($XDG_STATE_HOME/vigil/unit-heartbeats/vigil-settle-drain.service.hb.json) so the
# dead-man stands down. See vigil-alerts.env.example.
